        // But we must also ensure the call does not revert (e.g., target has no receive function but fallback).
        vm.assume(value > 0);
        // Use contract's own address to guarantee the fallback is invoked (it has no receive, so data triggers fallback).
        // But note: sending value to self via sendMoney will cause a recursive call that we don't want to count twice; we only care about the direct effect.
        // To avoid recursion, we can use a different address. Let's use a random address that we know will accept ETH (no receive, just fallback).
        // We'll use a fresh address.
        address payable dummy = payable(makeAddr("dummy"));
        // Deploy a minimal contract that has a fallback to ensure no revert? Actually any address with no code will accept ETH and not revert.
        // To avoid any issues, we can simply use an address with no code; it will still accept ETH without reverting on .call{value}.
        // But we need the fallback to be triggered on the *target*? Wait, the sendMoney function calls _target.call{value}(_data). If _target has no code, the call succeeds (no revert) but does not execute any fallback on our contract. That's fine: the fallback on our contract is not triggered by sendMoney, because sendMoney calls an external contract, not itself. 
